    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Choropleth maps in R with canvasXpress\"\ndescription: \"Build a rich, honest, interactive choropleth in R with canvasXpress — quantile classes and a viridis ramp that survive heavy-tailed data, plus the area-bias trap and sequential vs diverging palettes.\"\nauthor: \"Isaac Neuhaus\"\ndate: \"2026-10-06\"\ncategories: [R, canvasXpress, maps, choropleth, visualization]\nimage: \"thumbnail.png\"\n---\n\n\n\nA choropleth shades each region by a value — emissions, incidence, turnout, income. It is the most\ncommon map in data analysis and the easiest to get *subtly wrong*: most \"my map is all one colour\"\ncomplaints are not rendering bugs but scale bugs. This post builds a choropleth in R with\n[canvasXpress](https://canvasxpress.org) that stays vivid and honest on exactly the kind of\nheavy-tailed data that flattens naive maps. The join and the colouring happen inside the render, so\nevery figure is one portable spec you can re-run from R, Python or the browser.\n\n## First, the trap: colour encodes a *rate*, not a *count*\n\nA choropleth fills an **area**, and area is a size channel your eye cannot help reading. Shade\ncountries by a raw count — total cases, total emissions, total sales — and big, populous regions\nlight up simply because they are big and populous. You have drawn a population map with extra steps.\n\nThe fix is almost always to map a **rate or density**: cases per 100k, emissions per capita, sales\nper store. Reserve raw counts for a *proportional-symbol* map (a sized bubble at each centroid),\nwhere the size channel carries the count honestly. Decide which question you are answering before\nyou pick the geometry — this one choice prevents most misleading maps.\n\n## One value per region\n\nA choropleth needs exactly one measurement per shape. In canvasXpress the **columns** are the\nregions (they match the map by name) and the **row** is the variable you colour by — here CO₂\nemissions per country for 2018. Load a prepared table; swap it for your own (any `country → value`\ntable works).\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlibrary(canvasXpress)\n\ny <- read.table(\n  \"https://www.canvasxpress.org/data/r/cX-CO2-dat.txt\",\n  header = TRUE, sep = \"\\t\", quote = \"\", row.names = 1,\n  check.names = FALSE, stringsAsFactors = FALSE\n)\n\ndim(y)              # 1 row (CO2) x N countries\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n[1]   1 198\n```\n\n\n:::\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ny[, 1:4]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n    Afghanistan  Albania  Algeria Andorra\nCO2    10.45267 5.402999 164.3093 0.46421\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nThe single row is the variable (`CO2`); each column name is a country that must match a shape in the\nmap. An optional **sample annotation** table (rows = countries) rides along for richer tooltips and\nlegends — here the continent and ISO code.\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nx <- read.table(\n  \"https://www.canvasxpress.org/data/r/cX-CO2-smp.txt\",\n  header = TRUE, sep = \"\\t\", quote = \"\", row.names = 1,\n  check.names = FALSE, stringsAsFactors = FALSE\n)\nhead(x)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n            Code     Continent     Country\nAfghanistan  AFG          Asia Afghanistan\nAlbania      ALB        Europe     Albania\nAlgeria      DZA        Africa     Algeria\nAndorra      AND        Europe     Andorra\nAngola       AGO        Africa      Angola\nAnguilla     AIA North America    Anguilla\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## The map: quantile classes computed in R, painted by the map\n\n`graphType = \"Map\"` with a `topoJSON` of world boundaries draws the shapes and joins the countries\nby name — no manual merge, no projection math. The statistics stay in R, where they belong: we cut\nthe values into **seven quantile classes** with `quantile()` + `cut()`, so each class holds an equal\nnumber of countries (~28 of 198), then colour by that classed annotation with a viridis palette. On\nskewed data that equal-count guarantee is what keeps all seven colours on the map.\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nvals   <- as.numeric(y[1, ])\nbreaks <- unique(quantile(vals, probs = seq(0, 1, length.out = 8)))\ncls    <- cut(vals, breaks = breaks, include.lowest = TRUE)\nx$Emissions <- sprintf(\n  \"Q%d: %s\", as.integer(cls),\n  gsub(\",\", \" - \", gsub(\"[][()]\", \"\", levels(cls)))[as.integer(cls)]\n)\ntable(x$Emissions)   # ~28 countries per class\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n\nQ1: 0.00765 - 0.564    Q2: 0.564 - 2.69      Q3: 2.69 - 7.8      Q4: 7.8 - 22.4 \n                 29                  28                  28                  28 \n      Q5: 22.4 - 46        Q6: 46 - 208  Q7: 208 - 9.96e+03 \n                 28                  28                  29 \n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ncanvasXpress(\n  data      = y,\n  smpAnnot  = x,\n  graphType = \"Map\",\n  colorBy   = \"Emissions\",\n  topoJSON  = \"https://www.canvasxpress.org/data/maps/WORLD.json\",\n  mapId     = \"co2Quantile\",\n  colors = list(\"#440154\", \"#443983\", \"#31688e\", \"#21918c\",\n                \"#35b779\", \"#90d743\", \"#fde725\", \"#d9dde3\"),\n  mapColor        = \"#d9dde3\",\n  mapOutlineColor = \"#ffffff\",\n  legendPosition  = \"bottom\",\n  legendColumns   = 4,\n  title    = \"CO2 emissions per country (2018)\",\n  subtitle = \"Seven quantile classes computed in R: each colour holds about 28 of 198 countries\",\n  width  = 760,\n  height = 560\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div id=\"htmlwidget-f5120841cd0cc6fc003d\" style=\"width:100%;height:478px;\" class=\"canvasXpress html-widget\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-f5120841cd0cc6fc003d\">{\"x\":{\"data\":{\"y\":{\"vars\":[\"CO2\"],\"smps\":[\"Afghanistan\",\"Albania\",\"Algeria\",\"Andorra\",\"Angola\",\"Anguilla\",\"Antigua & Barbuda\",\"Argentina\",\"Armenia\",\"Aruba\",\"Australia\",\"Austria\